          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.783,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "MATHEMATICS"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.138,
            0.901,
            0.198
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider first a linear polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "ax + b, a \\neq 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . You have studied in Class IX that the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = a x + b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a straight line. For example, the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = 2 x + 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a straight line passing through the points "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( - 2 , - 1 )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and (2, 7)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.205,
            0.425,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.102,
                0.205,
                0.425,
                0.274
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_table_4.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>x</td><td>-2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>y = 2x + 3</td><td>-1</td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.293,
            0.425,
            0.604
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From Fig. 2.1, you can see that the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y \\; = \\; 2x \\; + \\; 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " intersects the x - axis mid-way between "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x   =   - 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x   =   - 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " that is, at the point "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left( - \\frac { 3 } { 2 } , 0 \\right)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " You also know that the zero of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x + 3   is   -\\frac{3}{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Thus, the zero of the polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 x \\; + \\; 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the x-coordinate of the point where the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = 2x + 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " intersects the x-axis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.444,
            0.212,
            0.897,
            0.534
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.444,
                0.212,
                0.897,
                0.534
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.633,
                0.553,
                0.715,
                0.573
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 2.1"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.614,
            0.901,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In general, for a linear polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "ax + b,   a \\neq 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = a x + b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a straight line which intersects the x-axis at exactly one point, namely, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left( { \\frac { - b } { a } } , 0 \\right)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Therefore, the linear polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "ax + b,   a \\neq 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , has exactly one zero, namely, the x-coordinate of the point where the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = a x + b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " intersects the x-axis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.748,
            0.902,
            0.841
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, let us look for the geometrical meaning of a zero of a quadratic polynomial. Consider the quadratic polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 2 }   -   3 x   -   4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Let us see what the graph* of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = x ^ { 2 } - 3 x - 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " looks like. Let us list a few values of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = x ^ { 2 } - 3 x - 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " corresponding to a few values for x as given in Table 2.1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_footnote",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.86,
            0.899,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "* Plotting of graphs of quadratic or cubic polynomials is not meant to be done by the students, nor is to be evaluated."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
